/thermal-shape-response-and-subtractions.md": "9657839ecc699cfd1be3f13ccc574178093282e664faae8aa02161b1cd1b4bbc", "notes/transported-clock-dirac-response.md": "77c4a4e6206b54b8f123015089fcae80b6b7fe9ed462014a9d0b57b53b889a48", "notes/tt-form-and-scalar-fourth-graph.md": "e1dc10ac0d6bbba23b47c431cc66fdf3eb99521bfb9e95e9e32c5567aa57bb5c", "notes/tt-mass-adjoint-certificate.md": "03967fae55d1d4a36c358c3cae499a42762c1592a12db93bafc589fb68876c33", "notes/tt-mass-numerical-bound.md": "9026b1269a63339e00bc69a2b1ea30ff7dc50878f2de1cc9b83e3c4ee33de844", "notes/uniform-dirac-reference.md": "a8214316cb9a7338680b55b92c446fa201c93f82f31dd0c7e94a2847296a361b", "notes/uniform-logarithmic-frequency-comparison.md": "c2e2cb216d45a508dcabe8ac0709f790f4631174ce32d40bfc4cbc62f5fbefc8", "notes/ward-sources-and-fast-compatibility.md": "fe77cf54525be57b0156c4c9d6e3f11e4ab41bfa1682b2a5b0f3b1bcaa54e8ba", "notes/weighted-normal-inverse-scale.md": "70c569775adce691d2d7e9b7a91a92591d43983bce64f1d287b22aeb4c48ea46", "notes/weighted-york-source-conversion.md": "4ed978cfaf69b04a01356d83619b9445b568eb945540a83e12b343e6d1212175"}, "frozen_sources_utf8": {"notes/current-complete-strong-response-attempt.md": "# Complete strong-response attempt and the remaining admission boundary\n\n2026-10-24, E003/S169. Ordinary argument before frozen verification.\nThe stable semiclassical wormhole objective is ACTIVE and INCOMPLETE.\nThe feasibility audit remains CLOSED.\n\nThis attempt assembles the remaining massive, nonlocal fast-graph response,\nincluding its physical returns. It does not construct a solution. The\nnumerical statement below has a specified admitted-family hypothesis; it\ndoes not establish admission of the original global nonlinear ball.\n\n## 1. Scope, first gap, and the exact operator being estimated\n\nThe previous checkpoint bounded the near cylinder-minus-cap return. It\nleft the returned logarithmic finite part, remote metric insertions and\nthe cap background seminorm unknown. A seed separated-kernel estimate\ncannot be differentiated to obtain their uniform metric bound. Likewise,\nthe near-near cap third variation is not the full variation: the metric\ninput also changes the propagator outside the localized source support.\nThese are the first unsupported strong steps addressed here.\n\nKeep the actual paired projector, complementary adjoint, regular disk\nbolt, prescribed state, thermal period, entire angular spectrum and ONE\ncommon proper-time regulator. Write q=Q_c/beta, ds/dz=1/q, sigma=4.005,\na=.01, bcut=.02, H=.005006. Q_c and the scalar extension are precisely\nthose of current-uniform-relative-domain-return.md. No physical wall or\nsmooth doubled metric is introduced. The cubic seam remains C2,1.\n\nFor the massive canonical response, retain the exact identity\n\n H_act,T = H_c,zero + H_c,thermal + (H_act,T-H_c,T),             (1)\n\non input z<bcut; input z>=bcut is treated on the actual geometry. The\nzero-winding term is a comparison in this identity, not a changed state.\nBoth the integral and the discrete frequency sum are bounded by the\npositive moment formulas below; all thermal terms in (1) remain present.\n\nThe graph input is u=z^sigma B_sigma^-1 g, ||g||H3<=1, on the original\nphysical interval, using the unchanged normal inverse on the full log\nline. Output is chi z^(5-sigma) partial_z H[u], in H3(dz/z). The scalar\nrow of this massive block is zero. All finite normal moments and the\nauxiliary normal tail are retained in its subtraction. Local geometric\nW4/a0/a2/LLL terms stay in their existing allocation. The equality of\nthe complete Taylor restoration plus conversion to that local allocation\nis the identity (4)-(6) of current-restored-frozen-response.md, including\nits boundary representatives. It is used once, before taking norms.\n\nHere eta_seed bounds this remaining seed block, and C3 bounds its first\nmetric variation with TWO fast graph inputs. C3 is not a bound for the\nentire gravitational nonlinear map or its independent mass/slow slots.\n\nThe admitted background family used in the estimate is explicit. On\n0<z<.02 let\n\n b=m z^3+z^4[d0+d1 log z+d2(log z)^2]+c_* z^(4+e_*)\n                                      +z^sigma B_sigma^-1 g0,   (2)\n ||g0||H3<=1e-11, 0<e_*<.01, ||b||A<=.001.\n\nA is the unchanged global value/collar three-jet/weighted fourth-jet\nnorm in the uniform-return note. Retain bounded admitted interior and\nbolt charts, identical fields on both copies, and their actual regular\ntraces. This includes the actual three slow powers without requiring\ntheir coefficients to be separately small, and allows the displayed\nquartic logarithms rather than silently setting them to zero. There is\nno estimate uniform over arbitrary log degree or an additional unbounded\nremainder. A proposed solution must be proved to have (2), or its extra\nphase seminorm must be added. Only the fixed-seed linear phase extraction\nis already established. Nonlinear preservation of (2) remains unproved.\n\n## 2. Closed domains and regulator hypotheses\n\nThe covariant D0 has complementary adjoint domains. The operator used\nfor accretivity is Dsharp=J D0: Dsharp*=-Dsharp on the specified paired\ndomain. Scalar bounded Lipschitz factors preserve both original domains;\ntheir squares are composed operators, not independently frozen Robin\nproblems. For A_M=Dsharp+M exp(b), its inverse and adjoint inverse obey\n||A_M^-1||<=exp(||b||infinity)/M. This verifies the massive gap used in\nthe relative heat argument. It would be false to infer it by calling\nthe original D0 skew-adjoint on its complementary domain.\n\nThe weak common-regulator conversion and its three graph-family metric\nvariations are those proved in current-conormal-common-regulator-attempt.md.\nIts hypotheses include bounded physical-side weighted seven jets and\nthe highest logarithmic graph derivative in L2. Equation (2) supplies\nthese: the fast inverse has H8,log control and the finite phase has its\nfixed degree. At the seam omega=log sqrt(q) has a NONZERO second jet and\na one-sided cubic jet; neither is set to zero. The balanced boundary\ncoefficient vanishes by L=S=0 and tr chi=0, not by smooth reflection.\n\nFor a relative localized comparison, apply finite propagation to\nK_M=[[0,A_M*],[A_M,0]]. It is self-adjoint, bounded Lipschitz scalars\npreserve its domain, its first commutator has norm at most the scalar's\nLipschitz constant, and its second commutator is zero. These are the\nactual hypotheses of [McIntosh--Morris, Theorem 3.1]\n(https://arxiv.org/pdf/1201.1818), inspected 2026-10-24. Its conclusion\nis finite propagation; it supplies no numerical trace or quantum norm.\nThe wave Gaussian and the massive gap then give, at EACH fixed annulus,\na relative differentiated heat majorant\n\n C t^-N(1+M)^N exp(-c1 M^2 t-d_h^2/(c2 t)), c1,c2>0.            (3)\n\nThe first-order local equation supplies the required finite spatial jets;\nthe explicit fourth mass vertex is included below. The complete angular\ncount makes (3) integrable at zero. At large time the mass gap applies.\nThis identifies the common relative limit. Constants in (3) are not used\nas a finite-cutoff numerical error allowance. The explicit Green bounds\nbelow give the numerical norms after identification.\n\nFor subtracted cap words, use the complete positive-cutoff Duhamel words,\nsubtract momentum degrees 0,2,4 together, and then the six-derivative\nM^-4 angular majorant. The background-order series is summable on its\nproved Wiener disk. Thus it identifies the weak finite part on each cap;\nthe strong inequalities extend that map by graph continuity. It does\nNOT assert operator-norm convergence of the raw cutoff with an\nunregulated normal subtraction. That argument remains disproved by the\npacket test in current-strong-response-decision.md. The weak identity\nand the uniform strong bound suffice for the metric fundamental theorem\nof calculus, first on dense inputs and then by continuity.\n\n[Vassilevich (5.33)](https://arxiv.org/html/hep-th/0306138v3#S5.SS3)\nprovides the smooth mixed-projector boundary polynomial only. Its use\nhere requires the separate rough-family extension just specified.\nThe Bessel representation [DLMF 10.32.9](https://dlmf.nist.gov/10.32.E9)\nis used only with Re argument>0; beta integrals have positive parameters;\ndigamma series are used away from their poles. None of these special\nfunction identities is a theorem about this rough quantum operator.\n\n## 3. The complete seed logarithmic return\n\nLet a_q=C/(30pi), K=GC beta^2/(15pi B^2), Omega=log(q)/2 and\n\n cA=log(beta)/2-L0-31/30-kappa4,\n kappaM=log(beta)/2-L0-6/5,\n kappaM-cA=kappa4-1/6, |cA|<5, |kappaM-cA|<16.\n\nAfter the already allocated local Taylor restoration, the frozen\nsubtracted symbol is F^2 A(p/F), with A=K_g-a_q kappa4 p^4. It is not\nthe geometric symbol inserted into an incorrect mass scaling formula.\nAt the seed, the returned logarithmic operator is\n\n q^-2[partial_s^4(log|D_s|+cA)-Omega partial_s^4]\n =q^2 partial_z^4(log|D_z|+cA)+q^2 Omega partial_z^4\n                                      +R'_4+(cA-Omega)R4,       (4)\n R_alpha=q^-2 |D_s|^alpha-q^(alpha-2)|D_z|^alpha.\n\nPrime on R denotes alpha differentiation. Equation (4) is an exact\ndistributional identity, not the substitution log|D_s|=log|qD_z|.\nIndeed the Riesz kernel is c_alpha |d|^(-1-alpha), where\nc_alpha=-Gamma(alpha+1)sin(pi alpha/2)/pi. Its analytic continuation\nhas c4=0 and c4'=-12 and retains its local delta derivatives.\nWriting y=z-d, the direct amplitude difference divided by d is\n\n E1=[q(z)^-2 q(y)^-1 M^(-1-alpha)-q(z)^(alpha-2)]/d,\n M=[s(z)-s(y)]/d.                                               (5)\n\nThe multiplier of d times the Riesz kernel is i alpha p|p|^(alpha-2).\nIts alpha derivative at four is i p^3[1+4log|p|]. In particular\nE1(z,0,4)=-(3/2)q q', giving +6q q' partial_z^3 in R4, in agreement\nwith q^-2(q partial_z)^4. Thus the lower contact is retained with its\nsign. The image amplitude is retained separately, including its seam.\n\nFor the actual Mellin return use\n\n |D_z|^alpha z^s = Gamma(s+1)sin(pi s/2)\n                 /[Gamma(s-alpha+1)sin(pi(s-alpha)/2)] z^(s-alpha).\n\nContinuation is made on the differentiated, moment-subtracted normal\noperator, not on a divergent undifferentiated integral at infinity.\nAt alpha=4 its derivative is\nP4(s)[psi(s-3)+(pi/2)cot(pi s/2)-log z] z^(s-4).\nWith P4=s(s-1)(s-2)(s-3), P5=(s-4)P4 and R=s(s-1)(2s-5), this gives\n\n flat logarithmic row = Q_M+Mlocal,\n Mlocal=K[(log z+kappaM-cA)P5+(s-4)R+P4].                       (6)\n\nThe flat Mlocal cancels against the retained explicit normal contacts,\nas in current-local-quantum-contact-graph.md, section 3. Its curved\ncoefficient (q^2-1)Mlocal does NOT cancel and is bounded here.\nAlso retain the density differentiation term\n\n -K chi z^(5-sigma)(q^2)' partial_z^4(log|D_z|+cA)u_cut.          (7)\n\nHere u_cut is the actual input restricted to z<.02. Its remote moment\nis finite. Replacing it in (7) by the growing auxiliary normal input\nwould create a divergent integral and invalidate the calculation.\n\nUse the whole-frequency constants Cb0,...,Cb5 of the local-contact note.\nThe polynomial multiplier guard is K sum |p_j| Cbj after expansion at\ns=sigma+it. Multiplication by chi z^p and chi z^p log z costs respectively\na^p M(p) and a^p[log(1/a)M(p)+M'(p)], where\nM(p)=p^3+12p^2+51p+100. These give the first two new allowances below.\nThe full logarithm q^2 log(q)/2 is enclosed through eight powers of q-1\nplus its geometric derivative tail through four Euler derivatives.\n\nFor completeness the coordinate remainder is estimated as a whole\nvariable-amplitude finite part. Use z-annuli h=.005*2^-j and H_z=.005\nin this paragraph's geometric sums and coefficient guards.\nTake output cutoff c_t on [-2,5], one on [0,3], and difference cutoff c_d,\none for |d/h|<=1/4 and zero for |d/h|>=17/4. Their transitions are the\nsame beta(9,9) polynomial, of widths two and four. For |alpha-4|<=1/2,\ncomplex disks of radius 6h bound E1 by\n6(U2 h+U3 h^2), including the complex-power phase. With T=21.25,\n\n delta=sum_(k=2)^6 |q_k|(T H_z)^k,\n U2=2|q2|T/(1-delta),\n U3=sum_(k=3)^6 k|qk|T^(k-1)H_z^(k-3)/(1-delta).\n\nThe guard follows by the segment-average formula for M and\nvmax=(10.25/2)(U2 H_z^2+U3 H_z^3); the denominator estimate is\n(1-delta)^(-5/2)[(1-vmax)^(-11/2)+(11/4)(1-vmax)^(-13/2)].\nCauchy supplies every mixed derivative needed below. The alpha derivative\ncosts two. Fourier Cauchy--Schwarz in output and difference variables\nuses one and five derivatives, respectively. If Ct_i,Cd_k are the cutoff\nguards, set\n\n A_j=12 sqrt(59.5) sum_(i=0)^1 sum_(k=0)^5 sqrt(binomial(5,k))\n     sum_(p=0)^(j+i) sum_(v=0)^k binomial(j+i,p)binomial(k,v)\n                 Ct_p Cd_v (j+i-p)! k_minus_v! /6^(j+i-p+k-v).\n\nHere k_minus_v!= (k-v)!. The order-three logarithmic kernel bound after\nr derivatives is\nsum_(j=0)^r binomial(r,j) A_j\n [7 I_(3+r-j),log S + I_(3+r-j)(32 S+4 SL)],\nwhere S=U2 S2+U3 S3, SL=U2 SL2+U3 SL3,\nSp=H^p/(1-2^-p), SLp=Sp[log(1/H)+log2*2^-p/(1-2^-p)].\nHere Sp and SLp use H_z. The Fourier moments used in these constants\nare (2pi)^-1 int (1+k^2)^-2 dk=1/4 and\n(2pi)^-1 int (1+k^2)^-2 log(2+|k|)^2 dk<1; the latter follows from\nlog(2+|k|)<=1+sqrt(|k|). Also |p-k|<=<p><k>. These give the displayed\norder-three kernel guards with room for the alpha derivative and its\nfinite contact. I_k and I_k,log are the full eta-localized input norms,\nobtained by the ordinary falling-factorial recurrence and the existing\nlogarithmic composition guards (181,8). Derivatives of cA-Omega are\nincluded in 32: on these disks |Omega|<.003 and its scaled derivatives\nare bounded by .003 j!/6^j. No derivative of a real kernel envelope is\nbeing taken. The checker implements this finite positive expression.\n\nIn the complementary direct support, |z-y|>=z/8; images have z+y>=z.\nFor complex output disks of radius |z-y|/4, the amplitude difference is\nat most 64[4|q2|max(z,y)^2+20max(z,y)^3]. The coefficient 20 encloses\nsum_(k=3)^6 |qk|1.5^k .02^(k-3). Including c4' and distance inflation\ngives 4096 times that polynomial, with derivative guard r!4^r. The\ncutoff derivatives contribute\nsum_j binomial(r,j)Cd_j(17/4)^j(r-j)!4^(r-j).\nSplit y<=2z and y>=2z, integrate the full power kernels, and use\n||z^p||L2(dz/z;z<a)=a^p/sqrt(2p). Every resulting p is positive.\n\nThe frozen spectral difference E has the entire angular count error\n|N(M)-2CM^2|<=4q_flux+2sqrt(C)M. Stieltjes integration and the Bessel\nrepresentation extend its separated bound to complex output disks;\nafter both density factors a safe coefficient is 4096, derivative\nr!16^r, multiplying q_flux beta |d|^(-3-r)+sqrt(C)beta^(3/2)|d|^(-4-r).\nUse the actual eta gap .24 and the same two-cone integrals. This\nincludes every angular level, direct and image terms, and the entire\nstationary time integral. Its diagonal extension is the previously\nbounded frozen symbol, not inferred from this separated estimate.\n\nThe numerical seed allowances from this complete return are:\n\n| Block | Upper allowance |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Retained (q^2-1)Q_M | .003138 |\n| Retained cap-minus-frozen | .000142 |\n| Retained near cylinder-minus-cap | .01755 |\n| (q^2-1)Mlocal | .003509 |\n| q^2 Omega partial_z^4 return | .000041791 |\n| Coordinate finite part near | 2.4e-8 |\n| Coordinate finite part far | 4.775e-18 |\n| Density term near/far | 2.248e-15 / 1.220e-27 |\n| Differentiated auxiliary normal tail | 1.446e-25 |\n| Frozen spectral near/far | 8.424e-10 / 7.517e-15 |\n| Remaining within-collar remote input | .00453 |\n| Actual physical input z>=.02 at the seed | 2e-6 |\n| Previous numerical reserve | 4.001e-9 |\n\nThe remote and near return entries are justified in sections 5-6. Their\nsum is less than .028913. Separate local action allowances are not\nsilently charged to this table a second time.\n\n## 4. Uniform cap seminorm, keeping the correlated phase\n\nFor h_j=H*2^-j let f_j=xi(s/h_j)(sqrt(q)exp(b)-1), with the established\nxi support [.25,3.5] and plateau [.5,3]. Define, as before,\n\n Bcap=sup_(b,j,4<=k<=8) h_j^(k-5/2) ||f_j||Hk,log\n                                      /[1+log(1/h_j)].          (8)\n\nThe physical factors removed in (8) are paid in the output sums. This\ndoes not improve a norm by renaming it. The old unweighted seminorm\nreally diverges for the seed quadratic term and is not reused.\n\nTake an outer cutoff zeta supported on [1/8,3.9], one on [.25,3.5].\nIts support satisfies 3.9 H*1.001<.02. The earlier exploratory endpoint\n4 did not satisfy that requirement and receives no certificate.\nSet a_t=zeta(sqrt(q)-1), b_t=zeta b, in scaled s coordinates.\nUse Hk,log<=2H(k+1) for the analytic seed and finite phase.\n\nThe finite phase in (2) satisfies\n(D-3)(D-4)^3(D-4-e_*)b_phase=0. Its A norm is at most .00105681:\nthe fast A debit is <=5.681e6*1e-11. Initial Euler jets through four\nare bounded by that A norm times 40^j z^3, by integration from the\nvanishing first two traces and z|b''''|. The recurrence continues this\nbound to every j because (1+4.01/40)^5-1<1. No inverse slow-root gap\nor separate large coefficients are used.\n\nFor actual coordinates use the exact recurrence\nP_(n+1)=q[(sigma-n+X)P_n+z partial_z P_n], P0=1,\nthrough eight for the fast part; replace sigma by zero through nine\nfor the phase. The seed analytic inverse has radius h/4 and amplitude\n300h^3+1e-8h^2. The scaled log map has derivative in [1/5,9]. Changing\nboth variables in its difference quotient gives Q-composition factor\n<1e4: the Jacobian/distance factor is 225, the small-distance logarithm\ncost is 1+log9/log2, and the remaining integral costs at most\n4*225*(log18)^2. The established norm equivalence gives the safe\nlogarithmic composition/product guards (2048;3000,16).\nFor the eighth derivative, integration by parts gives the pullback\nH^-1 guard 32W7, since 1/phi'<=5.01 and |(1/phi')'|<=1.01.\nIts multiplier commutator guard gives\n2048 M0 WL8+128(M0+M4)W7. Thus the top derivative stays in L2.\n\nLet Ha,Hb be these scaled H8,log guards divided by h^2 and Wa,Wb the\nWiener guards. Numerical substitution, uniformly in h<=H, gives\n\n Ha<7.718e18, Hb_fast<1.756e26, Hb_phase<9.244e15,\n Wa<.000410539, Wb<1.385e-8,\n Wxi<7.194, Hxi<9.218e15.\n\nAll scale powers in these positive guards increase with h, so H\nbounds every annulus. The entire exponential series obeys\nH(exp(b_t)-1)<=2H(b_t), W(exp(b_t)-1)<=2Wb: its n>=2 majorant is\nn^9 Wb^(n-1)/(log2 n!), with successive ratio <14Wb.\nThe H8,log product guard is 256[H(f)W(g)+H(g)W(f)]. On xi's support,\n\n f_j=xi[a_t+(exp(b_t)-1)+a_t(exp(b_t)-1)].\n\nScaling, product bounds and the retained reflected copy give\n\n Bcap <=512/log2 {Hxi[Wa+2Wb(1+Wa)]/h^2\n           +Wxi[(1+512Wb)Ha+(2+512Wa)Hb]} <2.062e30.            (9)\n\nThe computed allowance is 2.06181708695e30. Its contribution through\nthe previously proved coefficient 8.123e-26 is <167500 in C3, or\n1.675e-6 in the screening sum. The high-background loss therefore\ndoes not decide feasibility on (2). Without (2), or an explicitly\nbounded additional phase, (9) is not a bound on an arbitrary A ball.\n\n## 5. Complete cap variation, including remote metric slots\n\nThe all-angular Taylor-subtracted flat third kernel has the proved bound\n\n |Tsub|<=37C P^4 log(1+P^2/1296)/(5pi)\n                         <=74C P^4 log(2+P)/(5pi).              (10)\n\nIts three pair terms, both triangle orientations and common subtractions\nare included. The original fixed restoration is added as in section 1.\nApply the actual-coordinate recurrence, all binomial input derivative\nweights, output factors 4.04 c_(ell,r-1) and 2.02, cutoff guard 100,\nboth reflected copies and all Sp,SLp sums from the frozen-response note.\nFor k=4+r, replace its G_k coefficient by (74/(5pi))*2^k. This gives\n\n Cflat,near<7368340000,\n Ccap,near<=7368340000+521282000+8.123e-26 Bcap.                 (11)\n\nThe eighth input derivative contributes essentially all of the first\nterm. Using the coarser 9C symbol guard instead gives 14076682312.6\nfor that term. With the complete remaining ledger it fails the target.\nThat is a failure of this specified positive estimator, not a lower\nbound on the physical response. Equation (10) is a genuine sharper\nall-frequency inequality, with no normalization or physical mode cut.\n\nEquation (11) has both fast inputs localized by eta, supported on\n[.5,3] and one on [.75,2.5]. The actual cap family is F_j(b+lambda v),\nnot F_j(b+lambda eta v). Their difference has a remote metric slot.\nThe families agree on [.75h,2.5h], distance at least h/4 from the\nobservation interval [h,2h]. At lambda=0 they are identical.\n\nFor the source input within that common interval use the two-interface\nrelative Riccati formulas. Outside it use the sum of the two absolute\nbubble kernels, whose path from the observation has length >=h/4.\nThus equality of coefficients is NOT assumed on the entire source\nsupport. Coincident local Taylor terms cancel at the observation, but\nthe propagation seagull and all diagonal jumps are retained.\n\nThe original unit-disk argument gives parameter displacement\n.001/.499<.002005 and decay rate >1.98. In the fixed one-sided seed\ncoordinates the finite Taylor-germ flow and Lipschitz guards are\n14(M+p): p/beta<6.01p and the mass term is <5.5M. The exponent guard\nis <7.2(M+p). Radius 1/[160(M+p)] therefore keeps the total Riccati\ndisplacement below .1 and the two-arm inflation below exp(.18)<1.2.\nThis uses only the real output jets through three; the fourth mass\nvertex is explicitly added. No analytic rough field is assumed.\n\nUse the same summed local-L2 expression as the preceding joint-return\nnote, with cM=.99sqrt(10)/4, cp=.99sqrt(50)/4 and derivative factor\n160 in place of 400. Its 2^21 bubble and 2^11 seagull guards also\ncover the separated outside vertices. Input overlap is at most three.\nAdd .004H^3(K0 Vint+S0 V0) to the fourth output term. The resulting\nuniform local-jet allowance is .230015736742<.23002.\nCauchy in lambda and the proved fast A embedding give\n\n Cmetric,remote<.23002*(5.681e6)/.001<1306750000.                (12)\n\nThere is no seed debit from this difference, which vanishes at lambda=0.\nThe cutoff direction can have large derivatives away from observations;\nthose derivatives do not enter this estimate. The mass value controls\nremote propagators, and the original local jets control output and jumps.\n\n## 6. Source-input returns, including the actual bolt\n\nFor source input z<.02 outside eta, its proper gap is at least .24z.\nUse the actual/cylinder first-order finite-jet formulas through four\noutput derivatives; for the seed subtraction also include the frozen\nkernel. With d the physical z-distance, the complete mode moments give\n\n |partial_z^r K(z,y)|<=K_r d^(-5-r),\n K_r=8GC(1+gamma cp*.04)/(pi B^2) *2^23 r!400^r Z_(2,r),\n Z_(m,r)=sum_l binomial(r,l)(m+r-l+2)!l!\n                                  /[cM^(m+r-l+2)cp^(l+1)],\n cM=.99sqrt(10), cp=.99sqrt(50).                               (13)\n\nThe germ differentiates the full physical output density before taking\nabsolute values. It does not omit its zeroth-coordinate-return term.\nAt order four add the conservative 10K0/z vertex guard; its output\npowers are 3 and 7-sigma, both positive. Its allowance is <2.134e-22.\nFor the regular part, Mellin Schur integration uses\n\n I_r=(.76^sigma+2^sigma)(.24^(-4-r)-1)/(4+r)\n          +2^(sigma+1)/(4+r-sigma)+1/(sigma+1)+1/(4+r-sigma).\n\nTogether with W0, output cutoff 100 and the full derivative coefficients,\nthis gives .004524058188<.00453 and fast metric allowance <25740000.\nFor C3 this is the actual remote response itself; the frozen seed\nsubtraction is not spuriously differentiated or counted twice.\n\nThe fixed near actual-minus-cylinder term remains <=2.205e-13,\nwith fast metric allowance <.001253. The complete cylinder thermal\nresponse and fast metric response remain <1e-1000 at their proved\nscope. In the shrinking-cap comparisons (13) and (12), their positive\ndiscrete moment guard also bounds the zero-winding integral. Hence\n(1) keeps the entire thermal trace without requiring a new uniform\nthermal estimate for the shrinking caps.\n\nFor the ENTIRE actual source input z>=.02, the old seed bound is\n1.57870997615e-6<2e-6. Its neighborhood variation requires a new argument.\nNormalize the far input to supremum one and introduce a mass parameter\nmu of radius .001, supported there, and a metric parameter lambda of\nradius .001 with ||v||A<=1. The regular-bolt Riccati branch is holomorphic\nand remains in the unit disk for total radius <1/2. Its change at .02\nis bounded by two. Propagate this endpoint change through the COMMON\ncollar before taking the source derivative. No guessed bolt impedance\nor global frequency gap is used.\n\nThe combined displacement from the real branch is at most .002/.498.\nThe collar rate is bounded below by\n\n 2[1-(exp(.001)-exp(-.001))]\n -2exp(.001)(exp(.001)-1)-8(.002/.498)/sqrt5 >1.96.               (14)\n\nThe reflected branch gives the second propagation path. The local\nsource is proportional to M exp(b)(1-UV)/(1+UV), with the original\ndensity and physical-half factor. Its parameter difference has a safe\nguard 2^14, including density, reflection, denominator and germ costs.\nSet d=sqrt5(.02-.01), cM=.98sqrt2 d, cp=.98sqrt10 d, and\n\n S_r=8GC(1+gamma cp)/(pi B^2) 2^14 r!400^r Z_(1,r).              (15)\n\nDifferentiate the source in mu by Cauchy. This is exactly its far-input\nHessian; local contacts vanish by separated support, not by deletion.\nThe input supremum is at most 1801/sqrt2 times its graph norm. Sum\n100 sqrt(binomial(3,ell))c_(ell,r-1)S_r\na^(4+r-sigma)/sqrt(2(4+r-sigma)), r=1,...,ell+1,\nadd .002S0 times the fourth weighted power a^(7-sigma)/sqrt(2(7-sigma)),\nand multiply by (1801/sqrt2)/.001. This gives\n\n ||Hfar,b||graph->H3 <.24348,\n ||D Hfar,b||fast x graph->H3 <1383220000.                      (16)\n\nThe second bound uses Cauchy in lambda and 5.681e6. The smaller seed\nbound is retained for eta_seed; no derivative of that seed bound is used.\nAn initial exploratory coefficient 2^11 gave .030435; the certificate\nuses the larger explicit 2^14 source guard in (15).\n\nA sharp far input is only a bounded insertion in the fixed-domain mass\noperator. Approximate it by bounded insertions separated from outputs,\nuse the resolvent identity and (3), then take the dominated limit. No\nderivative is applied to its indicator and no discontinuous metric is\nsubstituted for the physical geometry. The regular bolt remains the\noriginal endpoint. Equations (14)-(16) also retain all discrete thermal\nfrequencies: the exact midpoint majorant is\ngamma sum p_j^l exp(-cp p_j)<=l!cp^(-l-1)(1+gamma cp).\nThe angular moments use N(M)<=4CM^2 and factorial integrals, never a\nfinite physical spectrum.\n\nDifferent cap identities are NOT glued into a discontinuous H3 map.\nDifferentiate each exact common identity locally, restrict its jets,\nand sum their L2 norms. The FULL response identities agree on overlaps;\nonly their sum is identified as the global H3 response. This avoids\ndifferentiating annular indicators or using a uniform W3 shrinking cap.\n\n## 7. Feasibility ledger and what remains unproved\n\nSubject to (2), the complete remaining nonlocal fast estimate has the\nfollowing sufficient ledger in the original normalization:\n\n| C3 contribution | Upper allowance |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Flat subtracted cap, actual coordinates | 7368340000 |\n| All-order cap difference, high inputs | 521282000 |\n| Cap high-background allocation | 167500 |\n| Retained near cylinder-minus-cap metric response | 99710000 |\n| Remote metric slot of the cap | 1306750000 |\n| Complementary within-collar input | 25740000 |\n| Actual entire far input, uniform metric response | 1383220000 |\n| Fixed near-domain/thermal remainder | .001253 plus 1e-1000 |\n\nThus eta_seed<.028913 and C3<1.0706e10 would give\n\n eta_seed+1e-11 C3 < .135973 < .1599164,\n screening reserve = .0239434.                                (17)\n\nThe conditional fast-sector test is therefore quantitatively affordable\nwith the displayed whole-spectrum estimator; it is a sufficient screen,\nnot a necessary condition for existence. Its dominant debit is the eighth\ninput derivative in (11), about .073684. The newly retained remote metric\nand entire far-input debits are about .013068 and .013833. The high\nbackground debit is <1.675e-6. Further cold/scalar optimization is not\njustified by these sensitivities.\n\nThe original norm comparisons and ball admission are NOT closed by\n(17). For a specified physical X, center offset Ecenter, input comparison\nalpha and output return RX, the unchanged sufficient requirements are\n\n Ecenter+1e-8 alpha<=1e-11,\n RX alpha*.028913+Z0_other<=.25,\n RX alpha^2*1.0706e10+Z2_other<=5e7.                            (18)\n\nThese are thresholds for this certificate, not necessary conditions for\nthe construction. Even with zero offsets/other rows they require\nalpha<=.001, RX alpha<=.25/.028913 and\nRX alpha^2<=5e7/1.0706e10. The combined mass/slow/global A allowance is\n<=.00094319 after the fast debit; it is unknown. The quantitative original\nX comparisons, joint affine data <=7.49e-12, known source <=1e-14,\nindependent Mellin far moment <=1e-10 and exact phase coefficients are\nnot supplied by the near strong operator estimate.\n\nThere is a real regularity restriction on possible comparisons. On a\ncompact interval inside the actual collar, take\nw_N=N^-5 psi(x)exp(iNx), with smooth nonzero compact psi. The physical\nfield z^sigma w_N has bounded C5 admission norm. But the actual full\nnormal symbol obeys B(sigma+iN)=K(iN)^5 log N(1+o(1)), so\n||B_sigma w_N||H3 grows as K N^3 log N. The original mass/bolt traces\nare zero for these compact test fields. Thus a bare C5 coefficient ball\nhas NO finite comparison alpha to this input graph. Changing units\ncannot fix it. This is a verified failure of that particular norm\ntransfer, not of a graph-strengthened physical solver or the construction.\nThe old audit already required an appropriate stronger graph/modulus.\n\nAt radius 1e-8, the original requirements remain\nY0+1e-8Z0+5e-17Z2<=1e-8 and Z0+1e-8Z2<1,\nwith allocations Y0<=5e-9, Z0<=.25, Z2<=5e7. Their source, local and\nnon-fast components are still unproved. Exact compatibility and the\ncomplementary equations, physical matching/causal realization,\nsemiclassical validity/noise and all-sector stability are unresolved.\nNo actual correction, controlled solution or wormhole is established.\nThe closed audit and the earlier sufficient projection screen remain\nat their previous scopes; the signed projection is not re-evaluated.\n\nRESEARCH DECISION: retain the construction and the common-restored graph\nmethod. Use an explicitly graph-strengthened global solver and direct\nphysical embeddings; do not try to transfer this estimate through a bare\nC5 ball. The quantitative fast-response screen alone is no longer a\nreason to change construction. Admission and the undifferentiated global\nmatching/source problem now precede further source optimization.\n\nSINGLE NEXT CALCULATION (PROPOSED, NOT EXECUTED): calculate the global\nsource-to-graph-and-phase return for the actual paired finite seed,\nretaining the undifferentiated Mellin moments that determine mass and\nthe correlated quartic/slow pair. Test the joint data target 1e-11 and\nremaining A allowance .00094319, with physical X-return constants meeting\n(18). This must include the finite geometry and bolt rather than solve\nonly the differentiated outer normal equation. It determines whether\nthe affordable conditional strong estimate can admit an actual correction.\n\nRemaining to-do list: global graph/phase source and return with original\nnorm comparisons; actual\ncorrection and nonlinear preservation/contraction; exact compatibility;\nphysical matching; validity/noise; all-sector stability; charge/return,\nopening, transport, preparation, separate creation and the full objective.\n", "checks/current_complete_strong_response_attempt.py": "\"\"\"Verify the complete admitted-fast estimate's arithmetic, not global admission.\"\"\"\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom hashlib import sha256\nfrom datetime import datetime,timezone\nfrom math import comb,factorial\nimport sys,json,traceback\nDEVELOPMENT='--development' in sys.argv\nif DEVELOPMENT:sys.argv.remove('--development')\nif '--arb-path' in sys.argv:sys.path.insert(0,sys.argv[sys.argv.index('--arb-path')+1])\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];sys.path.insert(0,str(ROOT/'checks'))\nfrom flint import arb,ctx\nimport current_product_matched_source_projection as old\nSTEM='current-complete-strong-response-attempt'\nOWN=['notes/'+STEM+'.md','checks/current_complete_strong_response_attempt.py','evidence/'+STEM+'-review.json']\nCHECKS=[]\ndef digest(p):return sha256((ROOT/p).read_bytes()).hexdigest()\ndef check(n,b):\n assert n not in {c['name'] for c in CHECKS}\n CHECKS.append(dict(name=n,passed=bool(b)))\n if not b:raise AssertionError(n)\n\nCORE=\"from pathlib import Path\\nimport sys,json,time,math\\nfrom functools import lru_cache\\nfrom math import comb,factorial\\n\\n\\nfrom flint import arb,ctx\\nimport sympy as S\\nimport current_product_matched_source_projection as old\\nctx.prec=512\\nroot=ROOT\\nseries=json.loads((root/'evidence/current-annular-response-feasibility.json').read_bytes())['runs'][-1]['result']['series']\\npars,_,_,_=old.scalar.prev.york.actual_parameters()\\nsig=arb('4.005');H=arb('.005006');C=pars['C'];beta=pars['beta'];B2=pars['B2'];g=pars['g']\\nq=[arb(1),arb(0),pars['u']/beta,(-4*beta-2*pars['u']-pars['tau'])/beta,(3*beta+pars['u']+pars['eps'])/beta,-2*pars['eps']/beta,pars['eps']/beta]\\nK=C*beta**2/(15*arb.pi()*B2);L=10*arb.pi()*(pars['Fl']+23*g/(180*arb.pi()))/g\\nCb=[2/(K*L*arb('.005')*sig*(sig-3)),2/(K*L*sig*(sig-3)),2/(K*L*sig),2/(K*L)]\\nCb4=max(Cb[0],8/(K*L),1/(K*(L*arb(6).log()).sqrt()))\\nCq=arb('2.328');W=Cb+[Cb[1],Cb[2],Cb[3],Cb4,Cq/(K*arb(2).log())]\\nWL=[max(W[j]*(2+arb('1e18')).log(),Cq/K*arb('1e18')**(j-8)) for j in range(8)]+[Cq/K]\\ndef rat(v):return arb(int(v.p))/int(v.q)\\ndef split(coeff):\\n rows=[coeff]\\n for i in range(len(coeff)-1):rows.append([(rows[-1][j]+rows[-1][j+1])/2 for j in range(len(rows[-1])-1)])\\n return [row[0] for row in rows],[row[-1] for row in rows][::-1]\\nbase=[S.Integer(0)]*9+[S.Integer(1)]*9;controls=[];current=base\\nfor order in range(10):\\n cells=[current]\\n for level in range(6):cells=[c for cell in cells for c in split(cell)]\\n controls.append([[min(cell),max(cell)] for cell in cells]);degree=len(current)-1\\n current=[degree*(current[j+1]-current[j]) for j in range(degree)]\\n@lru_cache(None)\\ndef M(order,offset):\\n power=sig+offset;vals=[]\\n if order==0:vals.append(arb('.75')**power if power<0 else arb('2.5')**power)\\n for cell in range(64):\\n  for left,width,idx in [(arb('.5'),arb('.25'),cell),(arb('2.5'),arb('.5'),63-cell)]:\\n   lo=left+width*cell/64;hi=left+width*(cell+1)/64\\n   bound=max(abs(v) for v in controls[order][idx])\\n   vals.append(rat(bound)/width**order*(lo**power if power<0 else hi**power))\\n return max(vals)\\n\"\nBLOCKS={'near': \"V0=arb(3).sqrt()*M(0,0)*W[0]\\nV1=arb(3).sqrt()*(M(1,0)*W[0]+M(0,-1)*(sig*W[0]+W[1]))\\nV=2*(V0*V1).sqrt()\\nPN=[{(0,0):arb(1)}]\\nfor n in range(8):\\n out={}\\n for (p,m),v in PN[-1].items():\\n  for j,qj in enumerate(q):\\n   if qj==0:continue\\n   out[p+j,m]=out.get((p+j,m),arb(0))+qj*(sig-n+p)*v\\n   out[p+j,m+1]=out.get((p+j,m+1),arb(0))+qj*v\\n PN.append(out)\\ncs=[[arb(1)]]\\nfor ell in range(1,4):cs.append([(5+j-sig)*(cs[-1][j] if j<ell else 0)+(cs[-1][j-1] if j else 0) for j in range(ell+1)])\\n@lru_cache(None)\\ndef bounds(i,offset,top=False):\\n r=sig+offset;guard=arb('1.001')**(r.abs_upper()+1)\\n m0=guard*M(i,offset)\\n m1=guard*(M(i+1,offset)+arb('1.01')*abs(r)*M(i,offset-1))\\n m4=arb(0)\\n if top:\\n  for j in range(5):\\n   order=4-j;bell=arb(1) if order==0 else arb(0)\\n   falling=arb(1)\\n   for k in range(1,order+1):\\n    falling*=r-(k-1)\\n    from sympy.functions.combinatorial.numbers import stirling\\n    bell+=abs(falling)*int(stirling(order,k,kind=2))*arb('1.01')**k\\n   m4+=guard*comb(4,j)*M(j,offset-4+j)*bell\\n return m0,m1,m4\\n@lru_cache(None)\\ndef part(k):\\n out=arb(0);by=[arb(0) for _ in range(9)]\\n for n in range(k+1):\\n  for i in range(n+1):\\n   for (p,m),cc in PN[n-i].items():\\n    power=sig+k-n+p;geo=arb(2)**(-power);sp=H**power/(1-geo);sl=sp*((1/H).log()+arb(2).log()*geo/(1-geo))\\n    mm0,mm1,mm4=bounds(i,-n+i+p,m==8)\\n    if m==8:aa=512*mm0*WL[8]+32*(mm0+mm4)*W[7]\\n    else:aa=725*mm0*WL[m]+9*mm1*W[m]\\n    term=arb(comb(k,n)).sqrt()*comb(n,i)*abs(cc)*(aa*sp+2*mm0*W[m]*sl)\\n    out+=term;by[m]+=term\\n return out,by\\nnear=arb(0);by=[arb(0)]*9\\nfor ell in range(4):\\n for r in range(ell+2):\\n  k=4+r;co=arb('2.02') if r==0 else arb('4.04')*cs[ell][r-1]\\n  outer=arb(comb(3,ell)).sqrt()*co*max(arb(1),arb(2)**(k-sig))*(arb(74)/(5*arb.pi()))*2**k\\n  val,bb=part(k);near+=outer*val\\n  for mm in range(9):by[mm]+=outer*bb[mm]\\npref=2*C*beta**2/B2*100*4*arb(2).sqrt()*V\\n\", 'background': \"# Positive auxiliary outer profile zeta: [.125,3.9], plateau [.25,3.5].\\nH=arb('.005006');AA=arb('.001');As=arb('.00105681');Efast=arb('1e-11')\\n@lru_cache(None)\\ndef Mz(order,offset):\\n power=sig+offset;vals=[]\\n if order==0:vals.append(arb('.25')**power if power<0 else arb('3.5')**power)\\n for cell in range(64):\\n  for left,width,idx in [(arb('.125'),arb('.125'),cell),(arb('3.5'),arb('.4'),63-cell)]:\\n   lo=left+width*cell/64;hi=left+width*(cell+1)/64\\n   bound=max(abs(v) for v in controls[order][idx])\\n   vals.append(rat(bound)/width**order*(lo**power if power<0 else hi**power))\\n return max(vals)\\nZeta=[arb(1)]+[max(rat(max(abs(a),abs(b))) for a,b in controls[i])*8**i for i in range(1,10)]\\nXi=[arb(1)]+[max(rat(max(abs(a),abs(b))) for a,b in controls[i])*4**i for i in range(1,10)]\\ndef H9_guard(caps):return 2*arb('3.875').sqrt()*sum((arb(comb(9,n)).sqrt()*caps[n] for n in range(10)),arb(0))\\nHxi=H9_guard(Xi)\\nWxi=(arb('3.875')*Xi[0]*Xi[1]).sqrt()\\ndef pn(sigma,number):\\n out=[{(0,0):arb(1)}]\\n for n in range(number):\\n  nxt={}\\n  for (p,m),v in out[-1].items():\\n   for j,qj in enumerate(q):\\n    if qj==0:continue\\n    nxt[p+j,m]=nxt.get((p+j,m),arb(0))+qj*(sigma-n+p)*v\\n    nxt[p+j,m+1]=nxt.get((p+j,m+1),arb(0))+qj*v\\n  out.append(nxt)\\n return out\\nPN=pn(sig,8);PS=pn(arb(0),9)\\n# Euler ODE for the retained slow powers and degree at most two quartic logs, without inverse root gap.\\nslow_der=[]\\nfor n in range(10):\\n guard=(arb('3.996')**(3-n) if n>3 else arb('4.004')**(3-n))\\n # For n>3 the minimum z/h is .124875, not the upper support endpoint.\\n if n>3:guard=arb('.124875')**(3-n)\\n slow_der.append(guard*sum((abs(v)*arb('4.004')**p*H**p*40**m for (p,m),v in PS[n].items()),arb(0)))\\nslow_profile=[sum((comb(n,i)*Zeta[i]*slow_der[n-i] for i in range(n+1)),arb(0)) for n in range(10)]\\nHbslow_over_h2=H9_guard(slow_profile)*As*H\\nEn=[sum((comb(n,i)*Zeta[i]*factorial(n-i)*4**(n-i) for i in range(n+1)),arb(0)) for n in range(10)]\\nHa_over_h2=H9_guard(En)*(300*H+arb('1e-8'))\\namp=300*H**3+arb('1e-8')*H**2\\nWa=(arb('3.875')*En[0]*En[1]).sqrt()*amp\\n# Total actual b, rather than separate large slow coefficients, supplies W(b_tilde).\\nb0=arb('4.004')**3/6\\nb1=arb('1.001')*arb('4.004')**2/2\\nWb=(arb('3.875')*b0*(Zeta[1]*b0+b1)).sqrt()*AA*H**3\\n@lru_cache(None)\\ndef bf(i,off,top=False):\\n r=sig+off;guard=arb('1.001')**(abs(r)+1)\\n m0=guard*Mz(i,off)\\n m1=guard*(Mz(i+1,off)+arb('1.01')*abs(r)*Mz(i,off-1))\\n m4=arb(0)\\n if top:\\n  from sympy.functions.combinatorial.numbers import stirling\\n  for j in range(5):\\n   order=4-j;bell=arb(1) if order==0 else arb(0);fall=arb(1)\\n   for k in range(1,order+1):\\n    fall*=r-(k-1);bell+=abs(fall)*int(stirling(order,k,kind=2))*arb('1.01')**k\\n   m4+=guard*comb(4,j)*Mz(j,off-4+j)*bell\\n return m0,m1,m4\\nHbfast_over_h2=arb(0);by=[arb(0)]*9\\nfor n in range(9):\\n for i in range(n+1):\\n  for (p,m),cc in PN[n-i].items():\\n   m0,m1,m4=bf(i,-n+i+p,m==8)\\n   val=(2048*m0*WL[8]+128*(m0+m4)*W[7]) if m==8 else 3000*m0*WL[m]+16*m1*W[m]\\n   term=Efast*arb(comb(8,n)).sqrt()*comb(n,i)*abs(cc)*val*H**(sig+p-2)\\n   Hbfast_over_h2+=term;by[m]+=term\\nHb_over_h2=Hbslow_over_h2+Hbfast_over_h2\\n# Whole e^b series: n=1 plus n>=2 bounded by n^9/(ln2 n!), ratio <14 Wb.\\nexp_tame=1+(arb(2)**9/(2*arb(2).log()))*Wb/(1-14*Wb)\\nassert Wb<arb('1e-6') and exp_tame<2\\n# W profiles divided by h^2 are increasing in h, retained explicitly.\\nWa_h2=(arb('3.875')*En[0]*En[1]).sqrt()*(300*H+arb('1e-8'))\\nWb_h2=(arb('3.875')*b0*(Zeta[1]*b0+b1)).sqrt()*AA*H\\nWprod_h2=Wa_h2+2*Wb_h2*(1+Wa)\\nBcap=512/arb(2).log()*(Hxi*Wprod_h2+Wxi*((1+512*Wb)*Ha_over_h2+(2+512*Wa)*Hb_over_h2))\\nans=dict(Bcap=old.encoded(Bcap),fast_H8log_over_h2=old.encoded(Hbfast_over_h2),slow_H8log_over_h2=old.encoded(Hbslow_over_h2),seed_H8log_over_h2=old.encoded(Ha_over_h2),Wb=old.encoded(Wb),Wa=old.encoded(Wa),Wxi=old.encoded(Wxi),Hxi=old.encoded(Hxi),exp_tame=old.encoded(exp_tame),by_fast_derivative=[old.encoded(x) for x in by],outer_support_inside_original_phase=bool(arb('3.9')*H*arb('1.001')<arb('.02')),scope='candidate uniform background bound on total A<=.001, fast graph radius 1e-11, and actual slow powers plus degree at most two quartic logs; global X admission still unproved')\\n\", 'coordinate_base': \"H=arb('.005');T=arb('21.25');delta=sum((abs(q[k])*(T*H)**k for k in range(2,7)),arb(0))\\nU2=2*abs(q[2])*T/(1-delta)\\nU3=sum((k*abs(q[k])*T**(k-1)*H**(k-3)/(1-delta) for k in range(3,7)),arb(0))\\nvmax=arb('10.25')*(U2*H**2+U3*H**3)/2\\nbound=(1-delta)**arb('-2.5')*((1-vmax)**arb('-5.5')+arb('2.75')*(1-vmax)**arb('-6.5'))\\n# IMPORTANT complex exponent modulus needs phase cost; using |alpha-4|<=.5 adds exp(.5 |log q|+...). Guard 6 replaces tentative 5.\\nCt=[arb(1)]+[max(rat(max(abs(a),abs(b))) for a,b in controls[i])/2**i for i in range(1,10)]\\nCd=[arb(1)]+[max(rat(max(abs(a),abs(b))) for a,b in controls[i])/4**i for i in range(1,10)]\\nA=[]\\nfor j in range(5):\\n aa=arb(0)\\n for i in range(2):\\n  for k in range(6):\\n   bb=arb(0)\\n   for pp in range(j+i+1):\\n    for qq in range(k+1):bb+=comb(j+i,pp)*comb(k,qq)*Ct[pp]*Cd[qq]*arb(factorial(j+i-pp))/6**(j+i-pp)*arb(factorial(k-qq))/6**(k-qq)\\n   aa+=arb(comb(5,k)).sqrt()*bb\\n A.append(2*arb('59.5').sqrt()*6*aa)\\nX=S.symbols('X');pol=[]\\nfor n in range(8):pol.append([rat(abs(v)) for v in reversed(S.Poly(S.prod(S.Rational('4.005')+X-j for j in range(n)),X).all_coeffs())])\\n@lru_cache(None)\\ndef input_norm(k,log):\\n out=arb(0)\\n for n in range(k+1):\\n  for i in range(n+1):\\n   for m,v in enumerate(pol[n-i]):\\n    if log:val=181*M(i,-n+i)*WL[m]+8*(M(i+1,-n+i)+abs(sig-n+i)*M(i,-n+i-1))*W[m]\\n    else:val=arb(3).sqrt()*M(i,-n+i)*W[m]\\n    out+=arb(comb(k,n)).sqrt()*comb(n,i)*v*val\\n return out\\ncs=[[arb(1)]]\\nfor ell in range(1,4):cs.append([(5+j-sig)*(cs[-1][j] if j<ell else 0)+(cs[-1][j-1] if j else 0) for j in range(ell+1)])\\ndef geo(p):\\n rr=arb(2)**(-p);sp=H**p/(1-rr)\\n return sp,sp*((1/H).log()+arb(2).log()*rr/(1-rr))\\nsp=U2*geo(2)[0]+U3*geo(3)[0];sl=U2*geo(2)[1]+U3*geo(3)[1]\\neta=arb(0);by=[]\\nfor ell in range(4):\\n for rr in range(ell+1):\\n  r=rr+1;outer=100*arb(comb(3,ell)).sqrt()*cs[ell][rr]*2**(4+r-sig)\\n  val=arb(0)\\n  for j in range(r+1):val+=comb(r,j)*A[j]*(7*input_norm(3+r-j,True)*sp+input_norm(3+r-j,False)*(28*sp+4*sl))\\n  eta+=outer*val\\n\", 'seed': \"H=arb('.005');a=arb('.01');bout=arb('.02')\\nxx,ss=S.symbols('t s');sigma=S.Rational('4.005')\\nTcross=(L/L.log()).sqrt()\\nCmult=Cb+[Cb4,max(Cb[0],2*Tcross*Tcross/(K*L),2/(K*(2+Tcross).log()))]\\ndef polyguard(expr):\\n poly=S.Poly(S.expand(expr.subs(ss,sigma+S.I*xx)),xx)\\n return sum((rat(abs(S.simplify(co/S.I**degree[0])))*K*Cmult[degree[0]] for degree,co in poly.terms()),arb(0))\\nP4=ss*(ss-1)*(ss-2)*(ss-3);P5=(ss-4)*P4;rr=ss*(ss-1)*(2*ss-5)\\nP5norm=polyguard(P5);P4norm=polyguard(P4);Rnorm=polyguard((ss-4)*rr+P4)\\ndef mul(p,v):\\n r=[arb(0)]*(len(p)+len(v)-1)\\n for i,x in enumerate(p):\\n  for j,y in enumerate(v):r[i+j]+=x*y\\n return r\\ndef add(p,v):return [(p[i] if i<len(p) else arb(0))+(v[i] if i<len(v) else arb(0)) for i in range(max(len(p),len(v)))]\\ndef DD(p):return [i*v for i,v in enumerate(p)]\\ndef Mpol(k):return k**3+12*k*k+51*k+100\\ndef Mp(k):return 3*k*k+24*k+51\\nq2=mul(q,q);rho=q2[:];rho[0]-=1\\nmr=sum((abs(v)*a**k*Mpol(k) for k,v in enumerate(rho)),arb(0))\\nmrl=sum((abs(v)*a**k*((1/a).log()*Mpol(k)+Mp(k)) for k,v in enumerate(rho)),arb(0))\\nnormal_local=P5norm*(mrl+16*mr)+Rnorm*mr\\n# phi=q^2 log(q)/2; full logarithm certified by a derivative tail.\\ndd=q[:];dd[0]-=1;dp=[arb(1)];ph=[arb(0)]\\nNN=8\\nfor n in range(1,NN+1):\\n dp=mul(dp,dd);ph=add(ph,[(-1)**(n+1)*v/(2*n) for v in mul(q2,dp)])\\nda=sum((abs(v)*a**k for k,v in enumerate(dd)),arb(0))\\nqa=sum((abs(v)*a**k for k,v in enumerate(q2)),arb(0))\\nphj=[]\\nfor j in range(5):\\n tail=qa*da**(NN+1)*(12+6*(NN+1))**j/(2*(NN+1))/(1-da*arb(12+6*(NN+2))**j/arb(12+6*(NN+1))**j)\\n phj.append(sum((abs(v)*k**j*a**k for k,v in enumerate(ph)),arb(0))+tail)\\nmp=sum((v*w for v,w in zip(phj,[100,51,12,1])),arb(0))\\nmdp=sum((v*w for v,w in zip(phj[1:],[100,51,12,1])),arb(0))\\nomega_local=mp*P5norm+mdp*P4norm\\n# Correct coordinate near estimate with the variable c_A-Omega amplitude.\\ncoordnear=arb(0)\\nfor ell in range(4):\\n for r0 in range(ell+1):\\n  r=r0+1;outer=100*arb(comb(3,ell)).sqrt()*cs[ell][r0]*2**(4+r-sig)\\n  val=sum((comb(r,j)*A[j]*(7*input_norm(3+r-j,True)*sp+input_norm(3+r-j,False)*(32*sp+4*sl)) for j in range(r+1)),arb(0))\\n  coordnear+=K*outer*val\\n# Physical density derivative: z^4-si